                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 917,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 836,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Swaps two axes of a tensor. For a 2-D tensor: rows become columns.\n\nThe default, axes 0 and 1, turns a 7 by 6 picture into a 6 by 7 one - row 0 becomes column 0, so the picture is flipped across its diagonal (the top-left to bottom-right line stays put). It is not a rotation: compare t.flip and t.roll on the t.roll page.\n\nFor a tensor with more axes you choose which two swap - [batch, time, joint] with transpose 1 2 becomes [batch, joint, time]. To reorder three or more axes at once, use t.permute.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.transpose <dim 1: int> <dim 2: int>\nDefaults: 0 and 1.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.transpose 0 1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor (a list or numpy array becomes a float32 tensor first). Receiving it makes the node send. A 1-D tensor (or a single number) has nothing to swap and is passed through unchanged.\n\ndim 1 / dim 2 (int boxes, also inlets):\nThe two axes to swap; order does not matter (0 1 and 1 0 are the same). The same axis twice gives the tensor back unchanged. Negative counts from the end. An axis the tensor does not have prints an error and sends nothing. Changing them does NOT send by itself; in the demo a metro inside make_F re-sends the F ten times a second.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\npermuted tensor out:\nThe tensor with the two axes swapped. Its memory is not moved, so the result is not contiguous: t.view refuses it (use t.reshape, or t.contiguous first).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe F (7 by 6) transposed lies on its side, 6 by 7: its top bar is now the left column and its stem the top rows. t.info shows the shape swap. Type 1 into dim 1 (so both are 1) and the F comes back unchanged.\n\nRELATED:\nt.t is the short form for 2-D tensors. t.adjoint swaps the last two axes of any tensor (and conjugates complex ones). t.permute reorders all the axes at once. np.transpose is the numpy version.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 820,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Swaps two axes of a tensor. For a 2-D tensor: rows become columns.\n\nThe default, axes 0 and 1, turns a 7 by 6 picture into a 6 by 7 one - row 0 becomes column 0, so the picture is flipped across its diagonal (the top-left to bottom-right line stays put). It is not a rotation: compare t.flip and t.roll on the t.roll page.\n\nFor a tensor with more axes you choose which two swap - [batch, time, joint] with transpose 1 2 becomes [batch, joint, time]. To reorder three or more axes at once, use t.permute.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.transpose <dim 1: int> <dim 2: int>\nDefaults: 0 and 1.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.transpose 0 1\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor (a list or numpy array becomes a float32 tensor first). Receiving it makes the node send. A 1-D tensor (or a single number) has nothing to swap and is passed through unchanged.\n\ndim 1 / dim 2 (int boxes, also inlets):\nThe two axes to swap; order does not matter (0 1 and 1 0 are the same). The same axis twice gives the tensor back unchanged. Negative counts from the end. An axis the tensor does not have prints an error and sends nothing. Changing them does NOT send by itself; in the demo a metro inside make_F re-sends the F ten times a second.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\npermuted tensor out:\nThe tensor with the two axes swapped. Its memory is not moved, so the result is not contiguous: t.view refuses it (use t.reshape, or t.contiguous first).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe F (7 by 6) transposed lies on its side, 6 by 7: its top bar is now the left column and its stem the top rows. t.info shows the shape swap. Type 1 into dim 1 (so both are 1) and the F comes back unchanged.\n\nRELATED:\nt.t is the short form for 2-D tensors. t.adjoint swaps the last two axes of any tensor (and conjugates complex ones). t.permute reorders all the axes at once. np.transpose is the numpy version."
                },
